<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thessaly</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thessaly is a region of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, a broad fertile plain ringed by mountains, including Olympus and Pelion. In antiquity it was famed for its horses and cavalry, and its cities, among them Larissa and Pherae, formed the Thessalian League. It later passed through Byzantine, Serbian and Ottoman rule and joined the Greek kingdom in 1881.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thessaly.jpg" alt="Thessaly" width="320" height="264" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thessalia_in_Greece.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…inflexible on broken ground: Philip V was defeated by Titus Quinctius Flamininus at the Battle of Cynoscephalae in <b>Thessaly</b> in 197 BCE, and Perseus' army was destroyed by Lucius Aemilius Paullus at the Battle of Pydna in 168 BCE, when Roman maniples exploited gaps in the advancing line.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Pelopidas extended Theban influence into <b>Thessaly</b> and Macedonia, and the young Philip II spent several years as a hostage at Thebes in the 360s BCE, where he probably observed Theban methods of war.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The youngest, Samuel, emerged as ruler, defeated Basil II at the Gates of Trajan in 986 and, ruling from Prespa and Ohrid, extended his power to Dyrrhachium, <b>Thessaly</b> and Duklja, taking the title of tsar around 997.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…its Balkan neighbours, shaped the wars that ended Ottoman rule in Europe, and its territory grew from the narrow state of 1832 to include the Ionian Islands (1864), <b>Thessaly</b> and Arta (1881), Macedonia, Epirus, Crete and the eastern Aegean islands (1912–13), Western Thrace (1920) and the Dodecanese (1947).</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom first lay along the Haliacmon and Loudias rivers north of Mount Olympus and along the coast of the Thermaic Gulf, bordered by Epirus, Illyria, Paeonia, Thrace and <b>Thessaly</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek continued to be spoken, dividing into dialects: Ionic and Attic in Athens, the islands and the coast of Asia Minor; Aeolic in Lesbos and <b>Thessaly</b>; Arcadian and Cypriot; and Doric in Sparta, Corinth, Crete and Rhodes.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
